abstract: >-
  A hypothesis and theory paper, with a reference simulator and pre-specified
  tests on open recordings. It proposes that experience requires signals
  between brain areas to arrive within a short window of a shared rhythm, and
  the connections doing so to span a quorum, a minimum share of the network;
  anaesthetics would remove such connections by transmission failure, timing
  variability or both. Simulations on three real connectomes show that the
  mechanism can operate, not that brains use it. Pre-specified open-data tests
  were inconclusive or could not be run; exploratorily, the functional MRI
  coupling network stayed intact through loss of responsiveness, by a
  descriptive criterion fixed in advance, on a measure that had little room
  to fall.
